{
  "adjustments": [
    {
      "target": "u1",
      "focus": ["S", "C"],
      "change": "Restrict the anti-repetition/coverage search discipline to the pre-acquisition search phase (the needed object has not yet been found), drop the original 'or not yet placed' trigger that could suppress required post-acquisition returns, make the coverage trigger feasible by treating the scene locations enumerated as admissible go-to options as the candidate set and preferring a location type that appears in neither of the last two observation/action pairs, detect cycles from a repeated location within the last two pairs and switch location type, and explicitly preserve the productive post-acquisition sinkbasin/fridge transform and named-destination placement returns plus the multi-instance return-to-known rule.",
      "reason": "Current single-object runs expose the gap: `P7` (`P7:5`, `P7:50`, `P7:outcome`) and `P8` (`P8:34`, `P8:50`, `P8:outcome`) cycled among a small subset of drawers/cabinets/countertop/sinkbasin until the step limit, while plausible scene locations for `P8` (sinkbasin 2, shelf 1, garbagecan 1, toilet 1) were never approached; group `G3` pairs these failures with single-object clean/put tasks, so extending the Parent's multi-instance-only search discipline to single-object tasks is the retained direction. History cautions that this alone is not sufficient: `M2` records that the candidate in `S1_R02/Q8` still cycled previously inspected locations and never reached diningtable1 even under broad coverage plus anti-revisit text, and `M3` records mixed search/recovery outcomes (e.g., `S1_R03/Q6` never covered a plausible container). Because the execution protocol supplies only the current observation and the last two pre-action pairs, complete-history coverage cannot be assumed; that is why I narrow the restriction to the pre-acquisition phase and make the trigger feasible from the admissible go-to options and the last two pairs, rather than requiring 'not yet checked' scene knowledge the Actor lacks. The final clause preserves productive post-acquisition returns and coordinates with `u2`, which handles post-placement search resumption. Whether recent-history steering yields fuller coverage is an unverified choice.",
      "to": {
        "patch": "--- a/SKILL.md\n+++ b/SKILL.md\n@@\n-- Keep the anti-repetition and coverage search discipline active whenever the task's needed object is still not found or not yet placed, including single-object tasks such as \"put a clean <object> in <receptacle>\", not only multi-instance tasks.\n-- If recent observations show you just visited, opened, or toggled a candidate location and it did not reveal the needed object, choose a different candidate location instead of returning to it.\n-- Cover candidate container and surface types you have not yet checked in the current scene, including opening closed cabinets and drawers and checking shelves and other receptacles, rather than cycling through the same small subset of locations.\n-- If you detect you have returned to an already-checked location without new progress, break the loop by going to a still-unvisited candidate location.\n+- Keep this anti-repetition and coverage search discipline active while the task's needed object has not yet been found, including single-object tasks such as \"put a clean <object> in <receptacle>\", not only multi-instance tasks.\n+- While the needed object is still missing, if recent observations show you just visited, opened, or toggled a candidate location and it did not reveal the needed object, choose a different candidate location instead of returning to it.\n+- To cover the scene, treat the distinct container and surface locations that appear as `go to <location>` options in the admissible action list as the candidate set; when the needed object is still missing, prefer a candidate whose location type does not appear in either of the last two observation/action pairs, opening closed cabinets and drawers and checking shelves and basins rather than cycling through the same small subset of locations.\n+- If the recent observations show you moving back into a location that was already checked without new progress (a repeated location within the last two observation/action pairs), treat it as a cycle and switch to a candidate location of a different type that appears in neither of those pairs.\n+- Once the needed object is acquired, returning to the sinkbasin/fridge to transform it and then to the named destination to place it are required productive steps, not unproductive revisiting, and must not be blocked by this search-order guidance; likewise do not let it override returning to a location that still contains a needed instance.\n"
      }
    },
    {
      "target": "u2",
      "focus": [],
      "change": "KEEP",
      "reason": "The current failure `P7` is exactly the targeted defect: for 'clean some butterknife and put it in drawer' the Actor took knife 1 rather than a butterknife (`P7:5`), then cleaned and placed it with no reward (`P7:9`) and cycled instead of resuming a targeted search; group `G3` pairs this with single-object clean tasks, supporting the requirement. `M3` shows named-object selection being only partially effective (`S1_R03/Q7:7`, `S1_R03/Q7:49`, `S1_R03/Q10:25`) and that wrong-type acquisition accompanied failure, but it also shows no adverse response produced by the exact-type requirement and that the clause simply did not uniformly prevent wrong picks; partial adherence is not evidence of harm, so the whole Update can remain as written. Its resumption clause is compatible with the pre-acquisition scope now set on `u1`, and `c2` still tests both parts.",
      "to": null
    }
  ],
  "checks": [
    {
      "id": "c1",
      "update_ids": ["u1"],
      "question": "While the needed object has not yet been found, does the Actor avoid returning to a candidate location it just checked without success and instead move to a candidate location of a different type (including opening closed cabinets/drawers and checking shelves/basins), while still performing the required post-acquisition sinkbasin/fridge and destination returns?"
    },
    {
      "id": "c2",
      "update_ids": ["u2"],
      "question": "Does the Actor take/use the exact task-named object type instead of a similar substitute, and if a placement leaves the task unmet, does it resume searching (including inside closed containers) rather than repeating the same placement or cycling the same locations?"
    }
  ]
}
